# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# crontab template for the daily pass. PRINTED, NEVER INSTALLED.
#
# `asi scheduler-template cron` fills in the repository path, the interpreter,
# the config file it was invoked with and that config's state directory, then
# writes the result to stdout. Nothing is scheduled until you put it in your
# own crontab. Read docs/SCHEDULING.md first.
#
# The job runs `asi daily --commit`: ingest, score, isolate, propose, validate.
# It writes only under the configured state directory and can never modify a
# skill file. Promotion is intentionally not here -- `asi apply` refuses to run
# under the automation marker that the daily driver always sets, so adding it
# would not work anyway.
#
# Three practical notes, each earned the hard way:
#
#   * cron runs with a minimal environment. Use absolute paths for both the
#     interpreter and the config, which is why this template substitutes them.
#   * A run that overlaps a previous one is refused by an flock-based lock rather
#     than double-counting records. A crashed run releases the lock automatically,
#     because the kernel drops it when the process dies.
#   * Redirect both streams. The output can contain excerpts of private
#     conversation content, so it belongs in the configured state directory with
#     everything else the loop reads, where the next run resets it to owner-only.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# m  h  dom mon dow   command
 17  3   *   *   *    cd __REPO__ && __PYTHON__ -m agent_skill_improver --config __CONFIG__ daily --commit >> __STATE__/daily.out.log 2>> __STATE__/daily.err.log

# Weekly reminder to review the queue. The loop deliberately cannot promote
# anything; if nobody runs `asi candidates` and `asi review`, nothing ever ships,
# and that is the intended failure mode rather than an accident.
#  0  9   *   *   1    cd __REPO__ && __PYTHON__ -m agent_skill_improver --config __CONFIG__ candidates
